// `validate_light_client_update`, answering which rule refused it.
//
// The order is the spec's, and the ordering is load-bearing in one place: participation and the slot
// relations are checked *before* any signature, so a malformed update is rejected without paying for
// a pairing. Everything here is supplied by an untrusted peer.
//
// ## The three checks that cannot be observed, and why the return type is the reason
//
// The shipped function's doc names its own weakest point, which is not something many do:
//
// > ## Three of these checks cannot be observed from outside
// >
// > Deleting any one of them changes no verdict on any input, and `test/sync_wac.test.ts` says so
// > rather than pretending otherwise — each is subsumed by a later cryptographic check … But **an
// > unobservable check is exactly the kind that rots unnoticed**, so it is named here as one.
//
// The three are `attested_slot >= finalized_slot`, the signature-period window, and
// `MIN_SYNC_COMMITTEE_PARTICIPANTS`. Each is subsumed: an update that fails one of them also fails
// the branch or the signature below, so both spellings answer `false`.
//
// **They are indistinguishable because the answer is a `bool`.** With a named reason, an update that
// violates the slot order answers `Err(SlotOrder(…))`, and the same update with that check deleted
// answers `Err(BadFinalityBranch)`. Two different answers, so the check is observable.
//
// Being precise about what that does and does not buy, because the difference is the whole of the
// claim:
//
//   * It does **not** make the existing vectors kill the mutant. The vendored updates are valid; none
//     of them reaches the check at all, and `Err` versus `Err` on inputs that never fail is still no
//     difference.
//   * It makes the distinguishing test **writable**. Today a hand-built update with the slots out of
//     order is rejected either way and no assertion can tell the two builds apart — so the test
//     cannot be written, not merely has not been. With a reason it is one `Err(is SlotOrder)`.
//
// So the type does not test the check; it stops the check from being untestable. That is a smaller
// claim than *the type fixes it* and a larger one than *the type prints better*, and it is the case
// this package makes better than any other here, because it did the work of finding out and wrote
// the answer down.
//
// **And the package already uses the other two techniques**, which is what makes the third group a
// gap rather than an oversight. Two checks the vectors also cannot see — `>= 2/3` and `>= 1/3`, where
// *"a safety threshold of `max/2` and of zero behave identically"* — were pulled out into
// `hasSupermajority`, *"a named function with its own boundary test at 21 and 22 of 32"*. That is the
// right answer for a threshold: extract and test directly. It is not available for a *rejection*,
// because a rejection's whole observable effect is the verdict — which is the thing a `bool` flattens.

import { Result, Bytes, Vec } from "core";
import { Chunk } from "@/packages/ssz";
import { provesFinality, provesCommittee } from "./branch.wac";
import {
  UpdateFault, NoBits, TooFewParticipants, MalformedHeader, SlotOrder, WrongPeriod, NotRelevant,
  BadFinalityBranch, BadCommitteeBranch, BadSignature,
} from "./fault.wac";
import { Store } from "./store.wac";

const i32 MIN_SYNC_COMMITTEE_PARTICIPANTS = 1;

/**
 * Whether `update` may be applied to `store`.
 *
 * `Ok` is *apply it*. Every `Err` names the rule, and the order of the checks is the spec's — the
 * cheap ones first, so an untrusted peer cannot make this pay for a pairing by sending rubbish.
 */
export Result<void, UpdateFault> validateUpdate(const Store store, Bytes update, i64 currentSlot,
                                                Bytes genesisValidatorsRoot) {
  Bytes bits = try aggregateBits(update);
  if (bits.len() == 0) { return Err(NoBits()); }
  i32 seen = countBits(bits);
  if (seen < MIN_SYNC_COMMITTEE_PARTICIPANTS) {
    return Err(TooFewParticipants(seen, MIN_SYNC_COMMITTEE_PARTICIPANTS));
  }

  Header? attested = headerOf(update, true);
  Header? finalized = headerOf(update, false);
  if (attested is null)  { return Err(MalformedHeader(true)); }
  if (finalized is null) { return Err(MalformedHeader(false)); }
  i64 attestedSlot = attested!.slot;
  i64 finalizedSlot = finalized!.slot;
  i64 signatureSlot = signatureSlotOf(update);

  // `current_slot >= signature_slot > attested_slot >= finalized_slot`, all four at once. One member
  // carrying all four, because a caller looking at a rejected update wants the numbers rather than
  // which of the three comparisons lost.
  if (!(currentSlot >= signatureSlot && signatureSlot > attestedSlot
        && attestedSlot >= finalizedSlot)) {
    return Err(SlotOrder(currentSlot, signatureSlot, attestedSlot, finalizedSlot));
  }

  i64 storeSlot = store.finalizedHeader.slot;
  i64 storePeriod = periodAtSlot(storeSlot);
  i64 signaturePeriod = periodAtSlot(signatureSlot);
  bool nextKnown = store.nextCommittee !is null;
  bool inWindow = nextKnown
    ? (signaturePeriod == storePeriod || signaturePeriod == storePeriod + 1)
    : signaturePeriod == storePeriod;
  if (!inWindow) { return Err(WrongPeriod(signaturePeriod, storePeriod, nextKnown)); }

  // Relevance: it must advance the finalized header, or supply the committee the store lacks.
  i64 attestedPeriod = periodAtSlot(attestedSlot);
  bool suppliesCommittee = !nextKnown && isCommitteeUpdate(update) && attestedPeriod == storePeriod;
  if (!(attestedSlot > storeSlot || suppliesCommittee)) {
    return Err(NotRelevant(attestedSlot, storeSlot));
  }

  // **Four lines each, because `try` cannot map.** `provesFinality` answers
  // `Result<void, ProofFault>` and this function answers `Result<void, UpdateFault>`; `try` requires
  // the callee's error to be *in* the caller's set and `ProofFault` is not — it is wrapped, for the
  // reason `./branch.wac` gives about not losing which branch failed. So the propagation that is one
  // token everywhere else in this directory is a `match` here, twice. See the note at the foot.
  match (provesFinality(finalizedLeaf(update), finalityBranch(update), attested!)) {
    Err(why): { return Err(BadFinalityBranch(why)); }
    Ok(_):    { }
  }
  if (isCommitteeUpdate(update)) {
    match (provesCommittee(committeeLeaf(update), committeeBranch(update), attested!)) {
      Err(why): { return Err(BadCommitteeBranch(why)); }
      Ok(_):    { }
    }
  }
  if (!verifies(store, update, genesisValidatorsRoot)) { return Err(BadSignature()); }
  return Ok;
}

/** A beacon header, as much of it as this walk reads. */
export struct Header { i64 slot; Bytes stateRoot; }

Result<Bytes, UpdateFault> aggregateBits(Bytes update) { … }
Header? headerOf(Bytes update, bool attested) { … }
i64 signatureSlotOf(Bytes update) { … }
i64 periodAtSlot(i64 slot) { … }
i32 countBits(Bytes bits) { … }
bool isCommitteeUpdate(Bytes update) { … }
Chunk finalizedLeaf(Bytes update) { … }
Chunk committeeLeaf(Bytes update) { … }
Vec<Chunk> finalityBranch(Bytes update) { … }
Vec<Chunk> committeeBranch(Bytes update) { … }
bool verifies(const Store store, Bytes update, Bytes genesisValidatorsRoot) { … }

// ## What could not be written
//
// **The three subsumed checks are still there and the reason for keeping them is now unstated.** The
// shipped doc keeps them for two reasons — *"each rejects for a few hundred nanoseconds what would
// otherwise cost a pairing"*, and not wanting this function's correctness to depend on
// `packages/bls` refusing an empty key list. Both survive here. What does not survive is the *third*
// sentence, that they are unobservable, because they are not any more — and nothing in this file
// records that they used to be, except this note. A rewrite that improves a property silently makes
// the next reader unable to tell the property was ever in doubt.
//
// **Answered, and not as expected.** This note used to say that `provesFinality`, `provesCommittee`
// and `verifies` all answer `bool`, that the collapse therefore happens one call down, and that the
// argument for going further *"does not obviously terminate"*. `@/packages/ssz` was written after
// that and the first two now answer `Result<void, ProofFault>` — see `./branch.wac`, which is the
// composition. It terminates: `ProofFault`'s members are the leaves, because below `verify` there is
// a loop of `sha256` and a comparison and a hash does not refuse. **A fault union ends where the
// next thing down cannot fail in more than one way**, which is a rule and not a judgement call, and
// which could not be found from this file alone.
//
// `verifies` still answers `bool`, and by that rule it should not stop there either: `packages/bls`
// distinguishes an empty key list, a point not on the curve and a pairing that disagreed. That is
// the next level and it is a different package again.
//
// **`try` cannot map, so wrapping a callee's fault costs four lines.** The two calls above are a
// `match` each, because `try` requires the callee's error to be *in* the caller's set and
// `ProofFault` is deliberately not — it is wrapped so that *which branch* survives. Everywhere else
// in this directory propagation is one token; the moment an error has to change shape it is a
// statement. `../../../QUESTIONS.md`.
//
// **Nothing says the slot relations are a chain.** `SlotOrder` carries all four numbers because a
// caller wants them, and the *type* still cannot say `current >= signature > attested >= finalized`
// — so a member with four `i64`s is a comment with fields. The alternative is three members, which
// answers *which comparison* and loses *what the numbers were*, and the honest reading is that this
// wants both: a rejection is a claim about an ordering, and an ordering is a relation over a tuple
// that this language has no way to name. `../../../QUESTIONS.md`'s first entry, from a fourth
// direction.
